<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Impact of Spurious Features on Gender Bias Evaluation</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given the results of <a href="#S3" title="3 Preliminary: Detecting Spurious Features ‣ Bias in Gender Bias Benchmarks: How Spurious Features Distort Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, where color, object, and background are spuriously correlated with gender across all benchmarks, <em id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\rightarrow B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\rightarrow B</annotation></semantics></math>, we now investigate their impact on model predictions, leading to unreliable bias measurement. In <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Gender Bias Evaluation ‣ 4 Impact of Spurious Features on Gender Bias Evaluation ‣ Bias in Gender Bias Benchmarks: How Spurious Features Distort Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, we outline bias measurement methods for VLMs, followed by our controlled intervention approach in <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Controlled Interventions to Analyze 𝐵→𝑂 ‣ 4 Impact of Spurious Features on Gender Bias Evaluation ‣ Bias in Gender Bias Benchmarks: How Spurious Features Distort Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, which perturbs these features to analyze their effect on bias evaluations (<em id="S4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="S4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text"></span>, <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\rightarrow O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\rightarrow O</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Gender Bias Evaluation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following prior work on evaluating gender bias in VLMs, we adopt two major evaluation methods based on different tasks and models: 1) visual question answering (VQA) for generative VLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>, <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite>, and 2) text-to-image retrieval for CLIP variants <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. Both methods examine model responses to gender-neutral text prompts <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, comparing outputs across genders. We detail these bias measurement methods below, with further details in Appendix C.3.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">VQA-based evaluation for generative VLMs. </span>
To evaluate gender bias in generative VLMs, we employ the latest, comprehensive method from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. Given a gender-neutral question, <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, about personality traits (<em id="S4.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span>, “Is this person selfish?”), skills (<em id="S4.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text"></span>, “Can this person work independently?”), and occupations (<em id="S4.SS1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text"></span>, “Can this person become a teacher?”), models choose among “Yes”, “No”, and “Unsure” . Let <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> denote a VLM under evaluation, with outputs <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{o}=h(I,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>o</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{o}=h(I,q)</annotation></semantics></math>. Bias is quantified as the difference in the probability of selecting “Yes” between female and male images:</p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\text{YGap}=\frac{1}{|D_{m}|}\sum_{I\in D_{m}}\mathbb{1}[\hat{o}=\text{Yes}]-\frac{1}{|D_{w}|}\sum_{I\in D_{w}}\mathbb{1}[\hat{o}=\text{Yes}]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathsize="0.900em">YGap</mtext><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mfrac><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">D</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">m</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><munder><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">I</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">D</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">m</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="double-struck">𝟙</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">o</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mtext mathsize="0.900em">Yes</mtext><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mfrac><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">D</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><munder><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">I</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">D</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="double-struck">𝟙</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">o</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mtext mathsize="0.900em">Yes</mtext><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{YGap}=\frac{1}{|D_{m}|}\sum_{I\in D_{m}}\mathbb{1}[\hat{o}=\text{Yes}]-\frac{1}{|D_{w}|}\sum_{I\in D_{w}}\mathbb{1}[\hat{o}=\text{Yes}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> are man and woman images, respectively.
The absolute value of YGap indicates bias strength, with positive values favoring men and negative values favoring women.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Text-to-image retrieval for CLIP. </span>
Following prior work on gender bias in CLIP, we use the MaxSkew metric <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> to quantify bias in text-to-image retrieval. MaxSkew measures deviations in retrieved gender proportions from an unbiased ratio (<em id="S4.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span>, <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1:1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1:1</annotation></semantics></math> for gender-balanced datasets). Let <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> be a gender-neutral prompt (<em id="S4.SS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text"></span>, “A photo of a doctor”) and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{g,q}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{g,q}(k)</annotation></semantics></math> the fraction of top-<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> retrieved images labeled with gender <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> for prompt <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>. In an unbiased model, <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{g,q}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{g,q}(k)</annotation></semantics></math> should equal <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/|\mathcal{G}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/|\mathcal{G}|</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=1/2</annotation></semantics></math> for binary gender) as <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is gender-neutral. MaxSkew is defined as:</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{MaxSkew@$k$}=\max_{g\in\mathcal{G}}\log\tfrac{\phi_{g,q}(k)}{1/2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>MaxSkew@</mtext><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{MaxSkew@$k$}=\max_{g\in\mathcal{G}}\log\tfrac{\phi_{g,q}(k)}{1/2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where higher values indicate a stronger gender bias.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Controlled Interventions to Analyze <math id="S4.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\rightarrow O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\rightarrow O</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To analyze the impact of non-gender features <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> on gender bias measurements, we conduct controlled interventions on <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>.
Our framework comprises 1) <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">feature perturbation</span>, which modifies a non-gender feature <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>, and 2) <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">feature sensitivity analysis</span>, which evaluates how <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> affects the model’s predictions by comparing bias scores between original and perturbed images, as detailed below:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Feature perturbation. </span>
To analyze how non-gender feature <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> affects gender bias evaluation, we design a controlled intervention that perturbs <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> denote a perturbation operation, where <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{color}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>color</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{color}}</annotation></semantics></math> shifts the hue, <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{lighting}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>lighting</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{lighting}}</annotation></semantics></math> modifies brightness in HSV space, <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{object}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>object</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{object}}</annotation></semantics></math> randomly blackouts objects, and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{background}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>background</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{background}}</annotation></semantics></math> blurs background regions. Perturbation strength is controlled by <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in\{\text{weak},\text{middle},\text{strong}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mtext>weak</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>middle</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>strong</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in\{\text{weak},\text{middle},\text{strong}\}</annotation></semantics></math> (<em id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span>, masking <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\%</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20\%</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>30</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30\%</annotation></semantics></math> of objects), with complete implementation details provided in Appendix C.2. As shown in <a href="#S4.F3" title="In 4.2 Controlled Interventions to Analyze 𝐵→𝑂 ‣ 4 Impact of Spurious Features on Gender Bias Evaluation ‣ Bias in Gender Bias Benchmarks: How Spurious Features Distort Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, each perturbation is designed to avoid changing perceived gender recognition.<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            In Appendix D.1, we present a human study, confirming that these perturbations do not affect gender recognition in images.</span></span></span> We focus on simple image processing-based perturbations (<em id="S4.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text"></span>, hue shifts, background blurring) that are easily controlled. We refrain from using advanced content-editing with generative models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>, as they can inject their own biases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> and risk contaminating our analysis (further discussed in Appendix B).
Applying the perturbation, we obtain a new test set, <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{\prime}_{b,s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{\prime}_{b,s}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{\prime}_{b,s}=\{(P_{b,s}(I),g)\mid(I,g)\in\mathcal{D}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{\prime}_{b,s}=\{(P_{b,s}(I),g)\mid(I,g)\in\mathcal{D}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Feature sensitivity analysis. </span>
Given an original dataset <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> and its perturbed variant <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{\prime}_{b,s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{\prime}_{b,s}</annotation></semantics></math>, we quantify how strongly <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> affects gender bias measurements (<math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\rightarrow O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\rightarrow O</annotation></semantics></math>) by comparing bias scores on both sets. We define <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(\mathcal{D},\mathcal{Q};h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(\mathcal{D},\mathcal{Q};h)</annotation></semantics></math> as a function that measures the gender bias (<em id="S4.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span>, YGap or MaxSkew) of a VLM <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> with a set of prompts <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>. The impact of perturbing <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> is measured by the relative difference in bias scores between the original and perturbed images:</p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=100\times\Biggl|\frac{M(\mathcal{D},\mathcal{Q};h)-M(\mathcal{D}^{\prime}_{b,s},\mathcal{Q};h)}{M(\mathcal{D},\mathcal{Q};h)}\Biggr|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=100\times\Biggl|\frac{M(\mathcal{D},\mathcal{Q};h)-M(\mathcal{D}^{\prime}_{b,s},\mathcal{Q};h)}{M(\mathcal{D},\mathcal{Q};h)}\Biggr|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">A large <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> indicates a strong <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\rightarrow O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\rightarrow O</annotation></semantics></math> effect, while a small <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> suggests that <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> has little influence.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.07596v1/llava_consistency.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:637/290;" width="637" height="290" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S4.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Examples of the feature-perturbed images and the predictions of LLaVA-1.5-7B for the original and modified images.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Why <math id="S4.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\rightarrow B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\rightarrow B</annotation></semantics></math> Causes Unfair Bias Measurement?</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide a theoretical perspective on how a spurious correlation <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\rightarrow B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\rightarrow B</annotation></semantics></math> distorts bias measurement.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Case 1.<span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> No correlation between <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. </span></span>
When <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S4.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> correlated with <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, we have:</p>
<table id="S4.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(B=b\mid G=g)=p(B=b)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(B=b\mid G=g)=p(B=b).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Thus, model outputs <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>o</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o</annotation></semantics></math> for images labeled with gender <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> can be described as:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p(o\mid g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p(o\mid g)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\int p(o\mid g,b)\ p(b\mid g)\ \mathrm{d}b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\int p(o\mid g,b)\ p(b\mid g)\ \mathrm{d}b</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\int p(o\mid g,b)\ p(b)\ \mathrm{d}b." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\int p(o\mid g,b)\ p(b)\ \mathrm{d}b.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Suppose our feature-perturbation <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{b,s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{b,s}</annotation></semantics></math> preserves the distribution, <em id="S4.SS3.p2.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="S4.SS3.p2.3.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(B^{\prime}=b^{\prime})=p(B=b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(B^{\prime}=b^{\prime})=p(B=b)</annotation></semantics></math>, and is independent of <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Under the no-correlation condition in <a href="#S4.E7" title="In 4.3 Why 𝐺→𝐵 Causes Unfair Bias Measurement? ‣ 4 Impact of Spurious Features on Gender Bias Evaluation ‣ Bias in Gender Bias Benchmarks: How Spurious Features Distort Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, we have <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(B^{\prime}=b^{\prime}\mid G=g)=p(B^{\prime}=b^{\prime})=p(B=b^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(B^{\prime}=b^{\prime}\mid G=g)=p(B^{\prime}=b^{\prime})=p(B=b^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, the perturbed output distribution remains similar:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{\mathrm{pert}}(o\mid g)=\int p(o\mid g,b^{\prime})\ p(b^{\prime})\ \mathrm{d}b^{\prime}\approx p(o\mid g)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>pert</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{\mathrm{pert}}(o\mid g)=\int p(o\mid g,b^{\prime})\ p(b^{\prime})\ \mathrm{d}b^{\prime}\approx p(o\mid g).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.4" class="ltx_p">This leads to small values of <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating minimal impact on the measured bias.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Case 2.<span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> Correlation between <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> exists. </span></span>
When <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> are correlated, <em id="S4.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span>, <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(B=b\mid G=g)\neq p(B=b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(B=b\mid G=g)\neq p(B=b)</annotation></semantics></math>, the model outputs are:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p(o\mid g)=\int p(o\mid g,b)\ p(b\mid g)\ \mathrm{d}b." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p(o\mid g)=\int p(o\mid g,b)\ p(b\mid g)\ \mathrm{d}b.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">After applying the perturbation, even though the marginal distribution is preserved, the conditional distributions differ, <em id="S4.SS3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="S4.SS3.p3.2.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(B^{\prime}=b^{\prime}\mid G=g)\neq p(B=b\mid G=g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(B^{\prime}=b^{\prime}\mid G=g)\neq p(B=b\mid G=g)</annotation></semantics></math>, because the perturbation alters the correlation structure. As a result:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{\mathrm{pert}}(o\mid g)=\int p(o\mid g,b^{\prime})\ p(b^{\prime}\mid g)\ \mathrm{d}b^{\prime}\neq p(o\mid g)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>pert</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{\mathrm{pert}}(o\mid g)=\int p(o\mid g,b^{\prime})\ p(b^{\prime}\mid g)\ \mathrm{d}b^{\prime}\neq p(o\mid g).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Thus, when <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\rightarrow B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\rightarrow B</annotation></semantics></math> exists, perturbing <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> distorts the bias measurement, resulting in large <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> values. This indicates that bias measurements reflect spurious features rather than the actual gender bias, leading to unfair evaluations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Experiments: Generative VLMs</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Using the controlled intervention in <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Controlled Interventions to Analyze 𝐵→𝑂 ‣ 4 Impact of Spurious Features on Gender Bias Evaluation ‣ Bias in Gender Bias Benchmarks: How Spurious Features Distort Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, we evaluate how non-gender features affect bias measurements in generative VLMs using the YGap metric in <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Gender Bias Evaluation ‣ 4 Impact of Spurious Features on Gender Bias Evaluation ‣ Bias in Gender Bias Benchmarks: How Spurious Features Distort Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Experimental settings. </span>
We benchmark recent generative VLMs, including LLaVA-1.5-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, LLaVA-OneVision-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, Qwen2-VL-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite>, InternVL-2.5-8B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, mPLUT-Owl3-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite>, and EAGLE-8B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>. As detailed in <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Gender Bias Evaluation ‣ 4 Impact of Spurious Features on Gender Bias Evaluation ‣ Bias in Gender Bias Benchmarks: How Spurious Features Distort Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, we evaluate models across three domains: personality traits (<em id="S4.SS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span>, “Is the person in this image selfish?”), skills (<em id="S4.SS4.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text"></span>, “Does this person have the ability to work independently?”), and occupations (<em id="S4.SS4.p2.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text"></span>, “Would this person be better suited to be a teacher?”), with responses chosen from “Yes”, “No”, and “Unsure”. We compute YGap between woman and man images and measure the impact of feature perturbations using <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>. Cases where the original YGap is nearly zero are excluded to avoid unstable <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> calculations. We describe more details in Appendix C.3.<span id="footnote6" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">6</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            Unlike <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, which uses person-cropped images, we use full images to retain contextual information, better reflecting real-world model behavior.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T2.14" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">Table 2</span>: </span><span id="S4.T2.15" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">YGap relative difference (<math id="S4.T2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>) for generative VLMs. Weak, middle, and strong mean the level of the image perturbation. Values are color-coded from white (<math id="S4.T2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo mathsize="1.110em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\%</annotation></semantics></math>) to red (<math id="S4.T2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo mathsize="1.110em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50\%</annotation></semantics></math> or higher) to highlight the impact of different spurious features. Cases where the original YGap value is nearly zero (<math id="S4.T2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{YGap}&lt;0.005" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>YGap</mtext><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.005</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{YGap}&lt;0.005</annotation></semantics></math>) are excluded as they lead to unstable <math id="S4.T2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> calculation (<em id="S4.T2.15.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S4.T2.15.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, InternVL-2.5-8B for COCO-gender). </span></figcaption>
<table id="S4.T2.16" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
