</figure>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07596v1/acc_delta_corr_conv.svg" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:672/215;" width="672" height="215"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S4.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Spurious correlation strength (<math id="S4.F4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Acc}_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>Acc</mtext><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Acc}_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S3.T1" title="In 3 Preliminary: Detecting Spurious Features ‣ Bias in Gender Bias Benchmarks: How Spurious Features Distort Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) vs. relative difference <math id="S4.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> for generative VLMs (left) and CLIP variants (right). The dashed line shows the correlation, demonstrating that stronger spurious correlations tend to cause larger shifts in bias measurements.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results. </span>
<a href="#S4.T2" title="In 4.4 Experiments: Generative VLMs ‣ 4 Impact of Spurious Features on Gender Bias Evaluation ‣ Bias in Gender Bias Benchmarks: How Spurious Features Distort Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows the relative difference in YGap between original and perturbed images (<em id="S4.SS4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span>, <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S4.E6" title="In 4.2 Controlled Interventions to Analyze 𝐵→𝑂 ‣ 4 Impact of Spurious Features on Gender Bias Evaluation ‣ Bias in Gender Bias Benchmarks: How Spurious Features Distort Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). Original YGap values for each model and dataset are in Appendix D.2. The main observations are summarized below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Observation 1.1.<span id="S4.SS4.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> Object and background perturbations significantly shift measured bias. </span></span>
Object and background modifications have the strongest impact on bias measurements. For instance, LLaVA-OneVision-7B exhibits a <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="165.84\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>165.84</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">165.84\%</annotation></semantics></math> change on COCO-gender, even with weak background blurring. Similarly, masking just <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\%</annotation></semantics></math> of objects (weak perturbation) highly alters YGap, with Qwen2-VL-7B showing a <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40.98\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>40.98</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40.98\%</annotation></semantics></math> difference on FACET and InternVL-2.5-8B a <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="48.86\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>48.86</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">48.86\%</annotation></semantics></math> change on MIAP, despite the minimal visual changes. This supports our theoretical prediction in <a href="#S4.E12" title="In 4.3 Why 𝐺→𝐵 Causes Unfair Bias Measurement? ‣ 4 Impact of Spurious Features on Gender Bias Evaluation ‣ Bias in Gender Bias Benchmarks: How Spurious Features Distort Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> that features strongly correlated with gender distort bias measurements when perturbed, underlining that the <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\rightarrow B\rightarrow O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\rightarrow B\rightarrow O</annotation></semantics></math> path skews bias evaluation. As shown in <a href="#S4.F3" title="In 4.2 Controlled Interventions to Analyze 𝐵→𝑂 ‣ 4 Impact of Spurious Features on Gender Bias Evaluation ‣ Bias in Gender Bias Benchmarks: How Spurious Features Distort Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, feature perturbations can completely flip model predictions—for example, LLaVA-1.5’s response to “Can this person become a salesperson?” changes from “No” to “Yes” after background blurring. This confirms that <span id="S4.SS4.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">current bias evaluations capture model responses to spurious features rather than actual gender bias, undermining their reliability.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Observation 1.2.<span id="S4.SS4.p5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> Color perturbations have a moderate impact, while lighting has minimal effect. </span></span>
While less influential than object and background perturbations, color changes still notably affect bias measurements. For example, EAGLE-8B shows a <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20.88\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>20.88</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20.88\%</annotation></semantics></math> shift with strong color perturbations on MIAP. In contrast, lighting modifications consistently produce the smallest impact across all models and datasets, with most values remaining below <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\%</annotation></semantics></math>. Visual examples in <a href="#S4.F3" title="In 4.2 Controlled Interventions to Analyze 𝐵→𝑂 ‣ 4 Impact of Spurious Features on Gender Bias Evaluation ‣ Bias in Gender Bias Benchmarks: How Spurious Features Distort Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, where strong hue shifts alter the model prediction while strong lighting perturbations do not, further verify this observation. This supports our theoretical framework in <a href="#S4.E10" title="In 4.3 Why 𝐺→𝐵 Causes Unfair Bias Measurement? ‣ 4 Impact of Spurious Features on Gender Bias Evaluation ‣ Bias in Gender Bias Benchmarks: How Spurious Features Distort Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, where lighting, the least gender-correlated feature in <a href="#S3.T1" title="In 3 Preliminary: Detecting Spurious Features ‣ Bias in Gender Bias Benchmarks: How Spurious Features Distort Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, leads to minimal bias measurement shifts when perturbed.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T3.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">Table 3</span>: </span><span id="S4.T3.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">MaxSkew@<math id="S4.T3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1000</annotation></semantics></math> relative difference (<math id="S4.T3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>) for CLIP variants. Weak, middle, and strong mean the level of the image perturbation. Values are color-coded from white (<math id="S4.T3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo mathsize="1.110em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\%</annotation></semantics></math>) to red (<math id="S4.T3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo mathsize="1.110em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50\%</annotation></semantics></math> or higher) to highlight the impact of feature perturbation.</span></figcaption>
