<div id="A4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#A4.T6" title="In D.2 Complete Results of YGap and MaxSkew ‣ Appendix D Additional Experimental Results ‣ Bias in Gender Bias Benchmarks: How Spurious Features Distort Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tables</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> and <a href="#A4.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ D.2 Complete Results of YGap and MaxSkew ‣ Appendix D Additional Experimental Results ‣ Bias in Gender Bias Benchmarks: How Spurious Features Distort Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, we show the complete results of YGap and MaxSkew@<math id="A4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1000</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. The results verify that feature perturbations highly affect the measured bias scores for both types of VLMs.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.T7" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A4.T7.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">Table 7</span>: </span><span id="A4.T7.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">MaxSkew@<math id="A4.T7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1000</annotation></semantics></math> results (scaled by <math id="A4.T7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math>) of the CLIP variants. Weak, middle, and strong mean the level of the image perturbation, and original means the results for the original images.</span></figcaption>
